## Description

This component represents a stateful multi-agent LLM workflow for automated Model-Based Systems Engineering (MBSE) in Software Defined Vehicle (SDV) development. The approach addresses cross-view inconsistencies and architectural hallucinations that can occur when Large Language Models independently generate structural, behavioral, and interaction models.

The workflow follows a sequential generation matrix consisting of Class, Activity, and Sequence diagrams. The Class diagram establishes the structural baseline of the system, including components, interfaces, methods, and vehicle signals. This validated architectural state is subsequently propagated to the Activity and Sequence diagram generation steps, constraining downstream model generation.

Vehicle Signal Specification (VSS) information is integrated through Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG), enabling the agents to retrieve standardized vehicle signals and their corresponding data types instead of generating potentially incorrect signal identifiers.

An independent AI Validator Agent performs semantic validation of generated models. The validator compares newly generated artifacts with previously validated architectural states and classifies detected problems according to a structured error taxonomy covering diagram misalignment, syntax and logic errors, hallucinated components, missing requirements, unintended deletions, and interface incompatibilities.

When inconsistencies are detected, a stateful backtracking mechanism enables the workflow to return to an earlier modeling stage. For example, if generation of a Sequence diagram requires an interface that is missing from the Class diagram, the workflow can return to the Class generation stage, update the structural model, and subsequently regenerate the dependent behavioral views.

The approach was evaluated using an automotive Child Presence Detection (CPD) ADAS scenario. The complete multi-agent workflow achieved 97% Entity Traceability, 87% Signal Conservation, and an F1-score of 85%, substantially improving cross-view consistency compared with zero-shot, RAG-only, and RAG with static validation configurations.

## Rationale

LLMs provide significant opportunities for automating MBSE activities, including generation of UML/SysML models from natural-language requirements. However, independently generated models can suffer from architectural drift, where components, signals, methods, parameters, or data types introduced in one architectural view are inconsistent with those defined in another.

RAG improves factual grounding by providing domain-specific information such as standardized VSS signals, but retrieval alone cannot guarantee structural compatibility between multiple generated models.

The proposed approach therefore combines domain grounding with persistent architectural state, sequential model generation, deterministic validation, semantic AI-based validation, and cyclic backtracking.

The structural model acts as the architectural foundation for subsequent behavioral and interaction views. Each downstream model is constrained by previously validated artifacts. When inconsistencies appear, the workflow can automatically revise upstream models and regenerate affected downstream views.

This provides a structured mechanism for reducing hallucinations and increasing traceability when generative AI is used for automotive systems engineering.

